===persian===

خواب... دریای... مه آلودِ... معطر،
و تو با، زورقِ ابر
هر شب از ساحل مهر،
در کنارم به سفر می‌آیی...
من و تو در صدفی می‌خوابیم...
عمقِ آبی...
دلِ سرخ...
و نَفَس‌های بنفش...
موج‌هایی می‌برد ما را...
تا دور...
تُو به تُو... دم در دم
پشتِ تاریکی‌ها... تا نور...
نه هراسِ ماندن...
نه شتابِ رفتن...
خواب دریای دل آرامِ صبور.
موج‌ها با هم در کار...
چشم‌ها بسته و دل بیدار...
من و تو در صدفی می‌خوابیم...
ساق در ساقی تر...
مثل دو نیلوفر...
تا سرِ شاخ سحر می‌تابیم...
«خوابَ رؤیای فراموشی‌هاست»*
خواب را دریابیم...
۱۳۷۴

===machine===

Sleep... a sea... mist-laden... perfumed,
and you, in a skiff of cloud,
each night from the shore of kindness,
come travelling beside me...
You and I sleep inside a shell...
blue depth...
red heart...
and violet breathing...
Waves are carrying us...
far out...
fold within fold... breath within breath...
behind the darknesses... to the light...
No dread of staying...
no hurry to be gone...
Sleep, the sea of the calm and patient heart.
The waves at work together...
eyes shut and the heart awake...
You and I sleep inside a shell...
stalk wound in wetter stalk...
like two water lilies...
we shine out to the very tip of dawn's branch...
"Sleep is the dream of all the forgettings"*
Let us take hold of sleep...
1374

===lantern===

Sleep — a sea, mist-laden, perfumed,
and you in a skiff of cloud
come travelling to my side each night
from the shore of kindness.
You and I sleep inside a shell:
blue depth,
red heart,
and violet breathing.
Waves are carrying us
far out,
fold within fold, breath within breath,
behind the darknesses, toward the light.
No dread of staying,
no hurry to be gone.
Sleep, the sea of the calm and patient heart.
The waves at work together,
eyes shut and the heart awake.
You and I sleep inside a shell,
stalk wound in wetter stalk,
like two water lilies,
and we shine to the very tip of dawn's branch...
"Sleep is the dream of all the forgettings"
Let us take hold of sleep...

1374

===translation===

===footnotes===

The line in quotation marks is not Jafari's. The printed page carries an asterisk and
answers it under the frame: **حمید مصدق** — Hamid Mosaddegh (1939-1998), the poet to whom this poem
is dedicated, and an exact contemporary. Jafari ends his own poem on a borrowed line and signs it
to its owner.

The phrase تُو به تُو, *fold within fold*, is printed here with its vowels marked. That matters
beyond this poem: the title poem on page 72 reads در دیوارهای توبه تو, which has always looked like
a typesetter's run-together of the same phrase, and this page shows the poet using it plainly.
